# Ctrl UI architecture

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this rule are to be interpreted as described in [RFC 2119](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119).

Product authority until planning is enriched: `docs/plans/2026-08-14-001-product-ctrl-ui-foundation-plan.md`. Language of committed prose: `.cursor/rules/english-language.mdc`.

## Product

Ctrl UI is an opinionated React + TypeScript design-system kit. It is not a headless library and not a copy-paste catalog.

Dependencies MUST flow downward only:

```
tokens → atoms → molecules → organisms → templates
```

A layer MUST NOT import a layer above it.

## Tokens

Tokens MUST use three tiers: primitive → semantic → component.

Components and consumers MUST use semantic or component tokens. They MUST NOT use primitive tokens directly. Visual values MUST come from the token pipeline, not literals.

## Customization

Public customization MUST use only these channels, in this order:

1. Semantic tokens / theme
2. Closed variant props (`intent`, `size`, `appearance` as finite enums)
3. Composition (compound components and documented slots)
4. `className` / slot class on documented nodes (last resort)

Public API MUST NOT expose `sx`, `backgroundColor`, or an arbitrary CSS-in-JS theme object as the primary styling path. Public API MUST NOT allow stripping semantics or keyboard behavior to make styling easier.

## Accessibility

Every public component MUST meet WCAG 2.2 AA, including Target Size 2.5.8 (24×24 CSS px minimum) and Focus Not Obscured 2.4.12. Overlay and composite widgets MUST ship focus management and keyboard behavior. Consumers MAY add ARIA. They MUST NOT remove the kit-owned contract.

## Toolchain (foundation)

| Concern | Required choice |
| --- | --- |
| Package manager | pnpm + Corepack |
| Library bundler | tsdown (ESM-first) |
| Catalog | Storybook on Vite |
| Tests | Vitest + Testing Library + axe |
| Lint | oxlint (no ESLint) |
| Format | oxfmt (no Prettier) |
| CSS lint | no Stylelint unless a hand-written CSS codebase appears |
| Git hooks | Lefthook + commitlint (Conventional Commits) |
| Versioning | Changesets + semver |
| Node | 22 |
| Module | ESM-only; React is a peer |

## Tests and coverage

The published kit source MUST be fully covered by Vitest. CI MUST fail unless coverage is **100% in every category**: statements, branches, functions, and lines.

Configuration MUST use Vitest `coverage.thresholds` with `100: true` and `perFile: true`. Coverage include MUST list kit source so uncovered files count. The RECOMMENDED provider is `istanbul` (stricter branch accounting for a 100% gate). `v8` MAY be used only if it is proven equivalent on this repo.

Coverage MAY exclude Storybook files, generated token dumps, and catalog-app scaffolding that is not published. Those exclusions MUST NOT hide kit source. A file is not "covered" by existing only as a story.

Do not mock components, hooks, or modules unless the dependency is an external boundary (I/O, browser API). Prefer real behavior.

### Test description convention

Use specification-style titles. This matches Vitest's own testing-in-practice guidance (names describe behavior, output reads as a spec) and BDD grouping without the noisy `should` prefix.

```ts
describe('Button', () => {
  describe('when loading is true', () => {
    it('exposes a busy state and ignores press', () => {
      // Arrange / Act / Assert
    });
  });
});
```

Rules:

- MUST use `describe` + `it` (not `test`) so titles read as sentences.
- `describe` MUST name the unit (`Button`, `resolveColorToken`).
- Nested `describe('when <condition>')` SHOULD group states and variants.
- `it('<observable outcome>')` MUST be third-person present tense.
- Titles MUST NOT start with "should", "test that", or "works".
- Titles MUST describe behavior and condition, not implementation (`calls setState`, `uses clsx`).
- Each `it` MUST assert one behavior.
- Test body MUST follow Arrange–Act–Assert.
- Colocate `Component.test.tsx` next to the source file.

## Storybook

The kit MUST be fully Storybooked.

Every public export (token sets, atoms, molecules, organisms, templates) MUST have CSF3 stories. Required stories for a component: Default, each public variant, and each meaningful state the public API exposes (disabled, loading, error, focus where relevant). Tokens MUST have visual stories, not only JSON.

Story `title` MUST follow Atomic Design (`Atoms/Button`, `Tokens/Color`, `Organisms/Table`). Autodocs and the a11y addon MUST run on stories. A public component without stories MUST NOT merge.

## Implementation plans

Any future implementation plan MUST use RFC 2119 keywords for requirements.

The plan MUST include this sentence near the top:

> The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

Use those keywords sparingly and only for real requirements or prohibitions. Body text that is not a requirement MUST NOT be capitalized as a keyword. Implementation plans MUST be English.
